+**D-69 Dynamic sampling for GRPO (DAPO-style; owner 2026-11-03, 385M run; code + CPU tests; default OFF in code, ON in rl_A_b64 / rl_B1_b256 / rl_C_mixed / rl_F_final, OFF in the preflight and in F2).** Module `gemma_mt/rl/dynamic_sampling.py`, keys `dynamic_sampling`, `dynamic_sampling_oversample` (1.1), `dynamic_sampling_max_refills` (2), `dynamic_sampling_min_std` (0.01). A group (16 samples incl. the greedy member) whose reward std is below `min_std` (floor 1e-6 = grpo's flat threshold; the SAMPLED members only with `greedy_stats: sampled`, the non-failed members with `hard_fail_mask: exclude`) has advantage 0, and a group with a tiny but non-zero spread is worse (`A = (r - mean) / (std + 1e-4)` turns jitter in the third decimal of a QE score into |A| ~ 1): such groups cost generation, scoring and backward passes for nothing. A step draws ceil(prompts x oversample) prompts, drops the flat groups, refills the deficit with ONE replacement prompt per dropped group (`PromptSchedule.replacements`: same language, same domain and length bin when the pool has one, so the schedule's language / domain / bin mixture is kept in expectation although the filter removes groups unevenly; a dropped group is replaced once, at most `max_refills` extra generation rounds, i.e. at most 1 + 2 generation calls per step) and updates on the first `prompts` informative groups; surplus informative groups are discarded (counted as waste). Fewer than `prompts` informative groups after the refills: the step trains on those (`ds/short_batch`); none at all: the first `prompts` groups of round 0 are kept (zero advantages, `ds/fallback`). MONITORING IS NOT FILTERED: reward, reward_std, hard_fail_frac, finished_rate, greedy_finished / loop rate, per-language statistics (D-65 window) and the translation archive are computed on ALL generated groups, because the filter removes exactly the all-failed groups and a kept-only finished rate (the early-stop / plateau input) would look better than the policy is; `completion_len`, `adv_*`, `weight_mean`, the update itself and the greedy advantage share use the kept groups. New record keys `ds/requested|generated_groups|kept_groups|dropped_flat|surplus|refill_rounds|short_batch|fallback|flat_frac|wasted_completion_tokens|wasted_token_frac|reward_kept|gen_groups_per_kept`, `ds_flat/<iso>` (the worst five languages of the step), running table `<out_dir>/dynamic_sampling_by_language.json` (generated / dropped_flat / surplus / kept per language, written every 10 steps). Step accounting: one optimiser step = one update on the kept groups, `save_every`, the KL guard, warm-up, extension and patience logic are untouched; the token loss is normalised by the kept tokens (flat groups no longer dilute it: the effective step is a little larger when many groups are flat, the KL guard watches it). Expected cost: `1 + flat share` generation work (+10 % oversample), measured flat shares in the preflight were 0.00-0.06, higher in weak / consensus languages. Tests `tests/test_dynamic_sampling.py` (12), `tests/test_dynamic_ref_loop.py` (4, CPU end to end, needs torch).
